Sort the items in each INSERT in PK order. This will probably prevent the deadlocks.
This will cause a deadlock if they start 'simultaneously':
INSERT (123,...), (987, ...);
and, from another thread
INSERT (988,...), (124, ...);
Assuming none of the values I picked are already in the table, each INSERT will grab a gap lock for the first value, then discover it cannot get a lock for the other. This is a 'classic' deadlock and is easily discovered. InnoDB picks one to kill.
If you simply rerun the killed transaction, it will probably succeed.
By sorting both INSERTs, the second becomes
INSERT (124,...), (988, ...);
The second INSERT to start won't even get started, so it will simply wait. No deadlock, just a minor delay.

